## Summary

This is the **Spring PetClinic REST** app (`spring-petclinic-rest`). The "business day" adjustment of the registration date lives entirely inside the **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** controller (there is no dedicated utility/calendar class in `src/main`). Notably, **production holiday handling does NOT exist** — the production code only rolls weekends. Holiday handling exists only in the *test* base class.

---

### 1. Business-day adjustment method (production)

**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`

`toBusinessDay(...)` — lines 564-569:
```java
private static LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate date) {
    while (date.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || date.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY) {
        date = date.plusDays(1);
    }
    return date;
}
```

`resolveRegistrationDate(...)` — lines 555-558 (single resolution point; defaults to `LocalDate.now()` when none supplied):
```java
private static LocalDate resolveRegistrationDate(LocalDate suppliedDate) {
    LocalDate date = suppliedDate != null ? suppliedDate : LocalDate.now();
    return toBusinessDay(date);
}
```

Callers of `toBusinessDay`: line 557 (via `resolveRegistrationDate`) and line 599 (`isBulkSignupWarning` → `countOwnersRegisteredOn(toBusinessDay(LocalDate.now())) > 80`).

Note: The production `toBusinessDay` only skips Saturday/Sunday — **it does not skip any holidays.**

### 2. Where the registration date is computed / set

Same file, `addOwner(...)`, lines 138-143:
```java
LocalDate suppliedRegistrationDate = owner.getRegistrationDate();
if (suppliedRegistrationDate != null && suppliedRegistrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);   // future dates rejected
}
LocalDate registrationDate = resolveRegistrationDate(suppliedRegistrationDate);
owner.setRegistrationDate(registrationDate);
```
The resolved date also feeds the membership-number year segment at line 440 (`generateMembershipNumber`, defined lines 575-578) and the per-day create-limit check at line 144.

The field itself is the JPA entity property:
**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`
- lines 67-68: `@Column(name = "registration_date") private LocalDate registrationDate;`
- getter/setter lines 180-186. It is also used in `deriveAgeBand` (lines 343-352) and a >365-day check (lines 310-311).

### 3. Holiday handling

There is **no holiday handling in production code.** It exists only in the test base class:

**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`
- `HOLIDAYS` set — lines 239-242 (hardcoded 2026 dates: 2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28).
- Test-side `toBusinessDay(...)` that skips weekends **and** those holidays — lines 267-274:
```java
protected LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate d) {
    while (d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY
            || HOLIDAYS.contains(d)) {
        d = d.plusDays(1);
    }
    return d;
}
```
There is a mismatch worth flagging: the test comment (AcceptanceTest lines 9-11) states "the default registration date rolls past public holidays as well as weekends," and the test base replicates holiday skipping, but the production `toBusinessDay` only skips weekends.

### 4. Related tests

**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (full file, 21 lines):
```java
/** holiday-business-day: the default registration date rolls past public holidays as well as
 * weekends. ... assert the invariant ...: the default registrationDate is never a Saturday or Sunday. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {
    @Test
